60151	Q0	zis363	19	3.846295	instruments_tools	The questionnaire documented here can be used in the context of course evaluations by students to collect students assessments of a seminar they have attended. In particular the questionnaire assesses the evaluation of the didactic skills of the instructor the perceived demands the perceived participation by other students the assessment of the materials used and the subjective learning outcome. Further items refer to the circumstances of seminar participation the overall assessment of the course and open feedback to the instructor. This instrument thus provides a comprehensive overview of how students experienced a seminar which aspects they liked and which they would suggest to change.
60151	Q0	zis262	20	3.749084	instruments_tools	The Cross Cultural Loss Scale CCLS a measure of loss associated with crossing national boundaries was developed across 2 samples of international students. With Sample 1 N 262 exploratory factor analyses were used to select the 14 CCLS items and to determine 3 factors Belonging Competency National Privileges and Access to Home Familiarity. With Sample 2 confirmatory factor analyses N 256 cross validated the 3 factor oblique model as well as a bifactor model. Cronbach s alpha and omega Brunner Nagy Wilhelm 2012 of both the CCLS total scale and the three CCLS subscales were acceptable to good and indicate that unweighted sum and mean scores can be computed. The validity of the CCLS scores was supported by its associations with related variables in the expected directions. Perceived cross cultural losses were positively associated with negative affect migration grief and loss and discrimination and were negatively associated with life satisfaction positive affect general self efficacy and social connection with mainstream society. Moreover the CCLS total and 2 subscale scores added significant incremental variance in predicting subjective well being over and above related constructs. The results indicated measurement invariance and validity equivalency for the CCLS scores between men and women.
60151	Q0	zis365	21	3.74893	instruments_tools	The concept addressed is perceived individual experiences of discrimination in everyday life as assessed by the Everyday Discrimination Scale. A German Arabic and Dari adaptation of the scale by Williams et al. 1997 is presented which underwent cognitive pretesting revision and validation in two samples a probability individual register sample for German and Arabic and a convenience sample recruited via Facebook for Arabic and Dari . The studies targeted the German speaking population and individuals with a migration background or refugees proficient in Arabic or Dari aged 18 years or older. Confirmatory Factor Analyses CFAs supported the unidimensional structure of the instrument. Validity and reliability analyses support its use within each language group and heterogeneous samples. Measurement invariance analyses showed that correlations can be compared without restrictions and means can be compared on the basis of partial invariance models.
60151	Q0	zis354	22	3.6923695	instruments_tools	Main status and employment are two complementary socio economic characteristics of a person. The main status describes social groups based on their current subjective main activity or status e.g. pupils self employed unemployed or retirees . It also describes an individual s position relative to the labour market which is closely linked to the life cycle. The main status strongly influences a person s everyday life behaviour experience and social relationships and is often used to select relevant subgroups for analyses. Gainful employment is conceptualised separately as it can not only be the main status as is usually the case with full time employees for example but can also be exercised part time alongside another main status e.g. students who work part time . In the case of output harmonisation of these characteristics only mediocre comparability can be expected as the measurement instrument in the questionnaire has a major influence on the response behaviour of respondents. It is therefore preferable to standardise the questionnaire items themselves.
60151	Q0	zis3	23	3.6647692	instruments_tools	The questionnaire covers the three areas of satisfaction stress and working atmosphere. The scale was developed according to the Lazarus stress model 1966 . The scale is scalable according to the Rasch model. The estimated reliability is very good .93 .94 . There are no indications for the validity of the scale.
60151	Q0	zis343	24	3.6175623	instruments_tools	Respondents age is together with sex gender the most important socio demographic variable recorded in all surveys. Age provides information about partly biologically determined development and ageing processes and positioning in the life course. It is therefore related to a variety of socio economic characteristics but also to attitudes behaviour and in particular health. It is controlled for in practically all multivariate statistical models. In most studies the age at the time of the survey is determined using questions on the month and year of birth and published as a derived variable. Membership in a birth cohort is also derived from the year of birth. It is used to depict similar overall social living conditions and experiences that lead to the construction of generations e.g. the post war generation or the baby boomers .
60151	Q0	zis232	25	3.5665548	instruments_tools	The scale captures different views on the meaning of life theism humanism and nihilism and was used in the General Population Survey of the Social Sciences ALLBUS 2012 among others.
